= Prior work and the contribution of this case
The earlier technical report, _Keating: A Metaharness for Agency-Preserving AI Instruction_, describes the teaching architecture, revision gates, and reproducibility checks @keating2026methods. Its evidence includes model-to-model traces and deterministic policy analyses. The present study supplies a complementary account of actual creator use: how explanations change during lessons, how the learner corrects the teacher, and how application development changes the encounter. Its distinctive contribution is the longitudinal relationship between learning a subject and building the system that teaches it.

== Learning while designing the teacher
Neustaedter and Sengers define autobiographical design as “design research drawing on extensive, genuine usage by those creating or building the system” #cite(<neustaedter2012>, supplement: [p. 514]). This is a close fit to Keating's creator learning with the application while revising it. A broken question input, a useful explanation, and a request for a missing animation all belong to the same experience. The creator's dual role gives the study access to instructional needs and repair work that a short, fixed-system usability session would miss.

== Explanations, correct answers, and later reconstruction
Self-explanation research provides a vocabulary for the changes visible in the embedding and Scheme lessons. In an experiment with 24 eighth graders studying circulation, students prompted to explain the text showed larger pre/post gains than students who read it twice @chi1994. The important distinction was between reproducing wording and constructing an account that connects ideas. Human-tutoring research likewise examined constructive learner contributions separately from acknowledgments and the amount of explanation supplied by the tutor @chi2001. In this case, a new inference or a precise objection reveals what the creator understands and gives the teacher material for its next response.

Correctness alone leaves part of that evidence hidden. Butler and colleagues tested multiple-choice answers together with confidence and subsequent answer production. Feedback improved retention even when an initially correct answer had been a low-confidence guess @butler2008. The implication for Keating is to follow a correct selection with feedback and a request for reasoning. The problem in the selected diagnostics is the tutor's decision to treat selection as completed understanding.

Research on explanatory depth adds a related assessment principle. Participants' ratings of their knowledge of familiar devices fell after they attempted detailed explanations @rozenblit2002. The Vulkan episode places the calibration problem in the teacher's judgment: Keating declares mastery while the creator continues to identify missing mechanisms. Requiring a mechanism-level explanation would make that judgment answerable to the learner's actual account.

“Testing is a powerful means of improving learning, not just assessing it,” write Roediger and Karpicke #cite(<roediger2006>, supplement: [p. 249]). In their prose-learning experiments, restudy produced better immediate recall, while retrieval practice produced better recall after a delay. That result gives the later SwiReasoning reconstruction a distinct place in this study and motivates scheduled follow-up assessments.

