= What comparative AI-tutoring studies establish
Recent experiments show why the instructional arrangement around a model matters. In a randomized crossover study of 194 university physics students, a structured AI tutor produced higher immediate post-test scores than an active-learning classroom condition: medians were 4.5 and 3.5 respectively @kestin2025. The tutor used prepared solutions, targeted feedback, self-pacing, and an interface that sequenced multipart problems. Keating's maps, questions, feedback, and interfaces similarly organize the teaching encounter; their reliability and sequencing are part of the pedagogical design.

A different result appears when assistance is removed. In a randomized study with nearly 1,000 high-school mathematics students, ordinary GPT-4 assistance and a teacher-informed GPT Tutor improved practice performance by 48% and 127%, respectively. On subsequent unaided exams, the ordinary GPT group scored 17% below control, while the GPT Tutor group was statistically indistinguishable from control @bastani2025. These findings make separate assisted and unaided measurements a central design requirement. In Keating's record, successful dialogue, correct selections, and later reconstruction therefore receive separate analyses.

Human oversight also changes the instructional arrangement. Tutor CoPilot supplied AI suggestions to human tutors in a randomized field study. Access increased the probability of passing a session exit ticket by four percentage points @wang2025. Keating places more of the oversight inside the learner's role: its creator challenges explanations, repairs interactions, and redirects lessons. Recording this corrective labor helps explain how useful instruction was achieved and what the application required from its user.

== From an observed problem to a tested revision
WikiSkill separates raw agent traces, accumulated knowledge, and deployable skills, then evaluates proposed updates through validation and rollback @tang2026wikiskill. Its experiments also examine interactions between skills and model capability. This supplies a systems precedent for preserving the creator's lesson evidence, recording a specific teaching hypothesis, and testing a revision across model configurations. The learning question then concerns what the revised teaching enables a person to explain, retain, and use.

The evaluator is part of that configuration. Zheng and colleagues report that absolute grading scores “are likely to fluctuate more than relative pairwise results if the judge model changes” #cite(<zheng2023>, supplement: [p. 4]). They also examine answer-order bias, verbosity, and reasoning errors. For Keating, recording the selected judge makes the scoring configuration explicit. Calibration compares its judgments against checked examples and human ratings. The faulty Scheme reference and premature mastery judgments supply concrete examples for that calibration set.

Together, these studies direct the analysis toward the work done in each exchange: the learner constructs an explanation, the teacher interprets it, the interface supports the next attempt, and a later assessment tests what remains. The case adds a record of that process as its creator, application, and underlying models develop together.
